# 0007 — Dolby Vision RPU passthrough for the hevc_nvenc encoder.
#
# PLATFORM `all`, unlike 0004. NVENC is built on every target: builder/scripts.d/50-ffnvcodec.sh
# emits --enable-nvenc, and its own header says the single linux pin "covers BOTH linux targets"
# because builder/build.sh:27 sources scripts.d/ whatever $TARGET is; msys2/ covers the two windows
# jobs. So hevc_nvenc exists in all four build artifacts and all four must carry these options.
#
# ⚠ 0001.checks argues the opposite and it does NOT apply here. It says "Do NOT collapse the two
# files into one `all`" -- but that is about 0001 and 0002 being two SEPARATE patches pinning two
# SEPARATE build systems, where one declaration each is what keeps them independently attributable.
# 0007 is a single patch in libavcodec that both build systems compile from the same source. The
# precedent that fits is `filter all tonemap_cuda`, an NVIDIA-dependent assertion already declared
# `all` in this repo.
#
# These three assertions prove registration: the options exist in the encoder's static AVOption
# table, so the patch applied and compiled in. They cannot prove the RPU is correct -- that needs a
# GPU and a Dolby Vision source, and lives in the patch header's verification record instead.
#
# The dv_l5 pair is declared by FF_DOVI_P81_L5_OPTIONS() in libavcodec/dovi_p81.h, shared with
# hevc_vaapi. If these names ever drift from 0004's, the macro is the thing that broke.

encoder-option  all  hevc_nvenc  dolbyvision
encoder-option  all  hevc_nvenc  dv_l5
encoder-option  all  hevc_nvenc  dv_l5_canvas
